    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d938">

                <pb facs="0728" n="612" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg612"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/13580.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to Special Agent
                        Canova.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-10-24">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">October 24, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your October 22, 7 p.m. Mr. Silliman’s telegram of October 7, 5 p.m., to Mr.
                    Fabela, is a restatement of the Department’s desires as expressed in its
                    telegram to the Brazilian Minister on September 22, 9 p.m., in which is clearly
                    set forth the wishes of this Government respecting the evacuation. If the
                    Convention has not a copy of the Department’s September 22, 9 p.m. to the
                    Brazilian Minister, Acting Foreign Minister Fabela should be asked by the
                    Convention to furnish such a copy.</p>
                <p>Repeat this telegram to Carothers as reply to last paragraph of his October 23, 9
                    a.m.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Lansing</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
